(a) Outdoor burning may not be conducted during any burning ban imposed for Paulding County by the Georgia Environmental Protection Division, which is typically from May 1 through September 30.
(b) This section is in addition to any restrictions or requirements imposed by state law as set forth in O.C.G.A. § 12-6-90, and as permitted/required by the Georgia Forestry Commission. Compliance with this code section is separate and distinct from any state law requirements regulating outdoor burning.
(c) Residential outdoor burning. Except for campfires, bonfires, and recreational fires within masonry or other prefabricated residential firepits, any person engaging in an outdoor burn shall comply with the following requirements:
(1) Prior to the fire and for each day of the fire, the person must call the automated outdoor burn registration system (770-505-2876) and listen to the entire recording;
(2) The fire must be attended at all times by an adult;
(3) The fire must be no less than the following:
(i) 50 feet away from any structure, including barns, outbuildings, sheds, and homes.
(ii) 25 feet away from any woodlands, forestland, and/or open field that contains brush, grass, or other flammable material.
(4) A garden hose, water supply, or extinguisher must be readily available at the site of the fire;
(5) Hand-piled material to be burned may not exceed (eight feet) x (eight feet) x (8 feet) in size.
(6) Only hand-piled natural products (i.e. leaves, tree limbs, etc.) may be burned. No household garbage or other hazardous materials (e.g. tires, plastics, etc.) may be burned;
(7) Machine-piled burns require notification to the Georgia Forestry Commission via the county forest ranger;
(8) Burning of any type of structure is prohibited;
(9) Burning is prohibited when the wind speeds or gusts are over ten miles per hour:
(10) All fires must be extinguished one hour before dark;
(11) Burning is prohibited when smoke presents a health hazard to persons in the vicinity of the fire or causes or may cause smoke or heat damage to structures or vehicles in the vicinity of the fire. Nuisance fires such as smoldering leaves or wet natural vegetation that produce an abundance of excessive smoke and affect the quality of life are prohibited.
(d) Campfires are only for recreational purposes or cooking food for immediate human consumption. A campfires is allowed both before and after dark. A campfire must be contained inside a ring, pit, or rock border and may be no larger than (three feet) x (three feet). Campfire burnings require clean materials only. Grass clippings or leaves may not be burned. A campfire must be at least 25 feet from any structure and attended at all times by an adult. A campfire shall be extinguished thoroughly when no longer in attended use.
(e) Bonfires are permissible for recreational purposes only and may only be conducted by civic, educational, religious, or other similar groups or organizations. A bonfire may be no larger than (six feet) x (six feet). A bonfire must be at least 50 feet from any structure and attended at all times by an adult. Bonfire burnings require clean materials only. Grass clippings or leaves may not be burned. A bonfire shall be extinguished thoroughly when no longer in attended use. A bonfire must have prior approval from the fire marshal, and when so approved is permitted only between the hours of 6:00 p.m. to 12:00 a.m. (midnight). Bonfire approvals will only be given to the property owner where the bonfire has been approved to occur.
(f) The fire chief may prohibit any and/or all outdoor fires when atmospheric conditions or local circumstances make such fires hazardous.
(g) Agriculture, silviculture, and land-clearing burns for residential or commercial development require notification to, and a permit from the Georgia Forestry Commission via the county forest ranger.
(h) As set forth in section 3.4.1 of the Development Regulations, any persons intending to burn for the purpose of land clearing must first contact the Georgia Forestry Commission at (770) 443-7821 for permitting purposes and must also comply with the requirements of state law pertaining to such burning.
(Res. No. 12-31, § 2, 9-25-2012; Ord. No. 21-06, § 1, 8-24-2021)

